# Copy to .env.local and adjust for your environment.
# The site runs with zero configuration; each block below upgrades one part of
# it from "works locally" to "production real".

# Canonical site origin (used for metadata, canonical URLs, sitemap, robots).
# Leave this unset locally: the site falls back to Vercel's per-deployment host
# on a preview deploy, and to http://localhost:3000 everywhere else. Set it to
# your own origin in the production environment only — it is the one value that
# stays the same across deploys, which is what a canonical URL needs.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://www.example.com

# Where "Log in" sends users (the banking app). Falls back to /get-started.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://app.example.com

# Contact addresses shown on the site. Each one is unset by default and every
# consumer — the contact page, the footer, Organization JSON-LD, the legal
# pages — renders nothing rather than inventing an address. Only set these to
# mailboxes that exist and that somebody reads.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_EMAIL=support@example.com
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SALES_EMAIL=sales@example.com
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PRESS_EMAIL=press@example.com

# Social profiles (footer). These are emitted in Organization.sameAs, which is
# a machine-readable claim to own the account. Set them only for profiles you
# actually control.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SOCIAL_X=https://x.com/yourhandle
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SOCIAL_YOUTUBE=https://www.youtube.com/@yourhandle
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SOCIAL_LINKEDIN=https://www.linkedin.com/company/yourcompany

# --- Regulatory disclosure --------------------------------------------------
# LEGAL: the site claims an authorisation ONLY when both of these are set, and
# they must match a real entry on the regulator's public register. Leave them
# empty until that is true; the copy then describes the licensed-partner model
# instead.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_REGULATOR_AUTHORITY=De Nederlandsche Bank
# NEXT_PUBLIC_REGULATOR_REFERENCE=R123456

# --- Database (submission storage) ------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. Both must be set together, or the app refuses to
# build a store and every form submission returns a visible error instead of a
# success screen. In development they are optional: with neither set, the app
# falls back to JSONL files under DATA_DIR so `npm run dev` needs no
# credentials.
#
# Before the first submission will succeed, apply the migrations in
# db/migrations/ once (`npm run db:migrate`, or paste them into the Supabase SQL
# editor in numeric order — see db/README.md). Without the `submissions` table
# PostgREST returns PGRST205 and every insert fails.
#
# SUPABASE_URL   — the project origin ONLY. Do NOT append /rest/v1; the client
#                  adds it (lib/postgrest.ts). A trailing slash is fine.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
#                — the *service_role* key, NOT the anon key. Row Level Security
#                  is on with no policies, so the anon key silently fails every
#                  insert. The service-role key bypasses RLS: server-side only,
#                  never expose it to the browser and NEVER prefix either of
#                  these with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# SUPABASE_URL=https://xxxxxxxxxxxx.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJhbGciOi...

# --- Customer accounts (/account) -------------------------------------------
# All three required together, on top of SUPABASE_URL above. With any of them
# missing, every auth page renders a setup panel instead of a form and the
# account area stays closed — which is a supported state, not a fault.
#
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY
#              — the *anon* (publishable) key, NOT the service_role key above.
#                This is deliberate and it is the whole security model: the
#                anon key carries no privilege, the signed-in user's own access
#                token supplies the identity, and Row Level Security in the
#                database is what decides whose profile row they can read. The
#                service-role key would bypass all of that.
#                Safe to expose in principle — but nothing in this app
#                authenticates from the browser, so do NOT prefix it with
#                NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# AUTH_SESSION_SECRET
#              — signs the session cookie. 32 characters minimum; use 64 hex,
#                e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`. Rotating it signs everybody out.
#                NEVER prefix this with NEXT_PUBLIC_: it is the one value that
#                would let anybody forge a session for any account.
#
# Before the first sign-up will work, apply db/migrations/004_auth_profiles.sql
# and set the two Supabase dashboard options that cannot be set from code — the
# redirect allow-list and the email templates. Both are in AUTHENTICATION.md,
# written to be followed without further research.
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOi...
# AUTH_SESSION_SECRET=64-hex-characters-of-randomness

# Directory for the development-only file store. Not a production path: it is
# never selected when NODE_ENV=production.
# DATA_DIR=.data

# --- Admin area (/admin) ----------------------------------------------------
# Both are required, otherwise /admin stays closed. Use a long random secret,
# e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-to-something-long
# ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=64-hex-characters-of-randomness
#
# Optional. Which generation of admin sessions is currently valid. Change it to
# any other value — a date is more useful than a number — to end every live
# admin session at the next request, without rotating ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET and
# without waiting for the 8-hour expiry.
#
# Rotate the *secret* when the key itself may have leaked. Bump this when a
# session may have: a stolen laptop, a browser left open on a shared machine.
# Conflating the two is what made revocation a redeploy. Defaults to 1.
# ADMIN_SESSION_VERSION=2026-08-19

# --- Email notifications via Resend (optional) ------------------------------
# When RESEND_API_KEY *and* RESEND_FROM are set, each new submission is emailed
# after it has been stored. Delivery failures are logged, never fatal — the
# record is always retrievable from /admin.
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# RESEND_FROM=Marsa <noreply@marsa.money>   # must be a Resend-verified sender
# RESEND_TO=team@marsa.money                # defaults to NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_EMAIL

# Override the FX data provider base URL if needed.
# FX_API_BASE=https://api.frankfurter.dev/v1
